1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,663
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,723 --> 00:00:09,911
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:10,624
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:14,891
Welcome to Unit 7: Exothermic and Endothermic Changes.

5
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:19,693
Nearly three billion people don't have clean fuel for cooking.

6
00:00:19,693 --> 00:00:25,396
Biogas digesters use bacteria to turn crop waste and animal manure into biogas, a cleaner fuel.

7
00:00:25,396 --> 00:00:30,148
Some changes release energy, and some take it in. Let's find out which is which.

8
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:36,435
When wood burns, it reacts with oxygen and releases its stored chemical energy as heat,

9
00:00:36,435 --> 00:00:40,594
warming the air around it. 'Exo' means out, and 'thermic' means heat.

10
00:00:40,594 --> 00:00:45,821
An exothermic reaction releases energy to the surroundings, so the temperature goes up.

11
00:00:45,821 --> 00:00:49,148
The products end up with less energy than the reactants.

12
00:00:50,208 --> 00:00:54,306
We can often notice the energy: a sparkler gives off heat and light.

13
00:00:54,306 --> 00:00:58,643
And when plaster of Paris is mixed with water, it reacts to form gypsum.

14
00:00:58,643 --> 00:01:02,801
A setting plaster cast feels warm, because the reaction releases heat.

15
00:01:03,861 --> 00:01:09,564
Water in a beaker is at 20 degrees Celsius. A spoonful of solid calcium chloride is stirred in.

16
00:01:09,564 --> 00:01:14,316
Predict: will the temperature go up, go down or stay the same? Pause and decide.

17
00:01:15,376 --> 00:01:18,643
Watch. The calcium chloride dissolves as it is stirred.

18
00:01:19,702 --> 00:01:23,682
The thermometer rises to 38 degrees Celsius. The beaker feels warm.

19
00:01:24,742 --> 00:01:26,049
Explain what you saw.

20
00:01:26,049 --> 00:01:32,227
Dissolving calcium chloride releases heat to the surroundings, so the temperature of the water goes up.

21
00:01:32,227 --> 00:01:38,286
A change that releases heat is exothermic. This is how some self-heating packs and hand warmers work.

22
00:01:38,286 --> 00:01:40,603
Pause and explain it in your own words.

23
00:01:41,663 --> 00:01:46,118
There are many exothermic reactions. Combustion is burning fuel in oxygen.

24
00:01:46,118 --> 00:01:50,573
Neutralisation is an acid reacting with an alkali to make a salt and water.

25
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:55,613
Hydration is a substance reacting with water, like cement setting.

26
00:01:55,613 --> 00:01:59,712
Reactive metals reacting with water, like sodium, are exothermic too.

27
00:02:00,771 --> 00:02:06,593
Rusting releases heat slowly. And aerobic respiration in your cells releases energy from glucose.

28
00:02:06,593 --> 00:02:12,415
Remember, a reaction doesn't always make one pure product: often it makes a mixture of substances.

29
00:02:13,474 --> 00:02:18,464
An exothermic process is any change that releases heat, including physical changes.

30
00:02:18,464 --> 00:02:24,642
When water freezes, its particles lose energy, slow down and lock into fixed positions, releasing heat.

31
00:02:24,642 --> 00:02:28,147
No new substance is formed, and the change can be reversed.

32
00:02:29,207 --> 00:02:34,910
Dissolving some substances, like calcium chloride or solid sodium hydroxide, releases heat too,

33
00:02:34,910 --> 00:02:39,721
so the solution feels warm. Instant heat packs for sports injuries work this way.

34
00:02:40,781 --> 00:02:46,009
Crystallisation, when crystals form from a supersaturated solution, is also exothermic.

35
00:02:46,009 --> 00:02:51,355
Press a reusable hand warmer, and it crystallises, heating up to about 54 degrees Celsius.
